claims.statement:
Measured from the repo's circuits (one ancilla per check). XYZ^2 needs more hardware on every axis:
- about twice the qubits;
- 2.08-2.25x the two-qubit gates per round;
- 8 ticks per round against 6;
- coupler degree 6 against 4 (this circuit);
- three gate types against two.

At equal qubits (99/97) the two-qubit gate count is the same (170 vs 168), but idle exposure is not:
- Gate-layer idle sites per round: 254 vs 52 (4.9x). XYZ^2's 254 split into 130 on data qubits, 100 on link ancillas (idle in 4 of 6 layers) and 24 on plaquette ancillas.
- Reset and readout-window sites: 100 vs 98.
- Total, the SD6-style count: 354 vs 150 (2.4x).
So any gate-layer dephasing charges XYZ^2 about 4.9x more (C37). About half of that is ancilla waiting, which a just-in-time link-ancilla schedule might cut (untested).

The XZZX and CSS d = 3 coupling graphs are not subgraphs of XYZ^2's d = 3 or d = 5 graphs. The 'same hardware' premise is exact only for all-to-all or reconfigurable platforms.

Overhead by layout:
- 2x with one ancilla per check;
- 1.5x hybrid (2112.06036's triangular proposal, 3d^2-1);
- about 1.6-1.7x under em3 (355 vs 209 declared qubits at d = 5);
- 1.83x pipelined (266 vs 146 at d = 7);
- 1.33x in Hetenyi-Wootton's square-lattice spin layout (4d^2 vs 3d^2, connectivity 4, no SWAPs for links). There the links reuse plaquette ancilla pairs and are read in a separate round, so data idle through two readout windows per cycle. A one-window layout needs about 6d^2, i.e. 2x (inference);
- 0.67x against Floquet codes in their layout.

Measured from the code. The biased models put Z-type channels (IZ, ZI, ZZ) after every native CX, CY and CZ, so they preserve bias; measurement and reset flips stay at p.

Three files list XYZ^2's biased values under 'gates not bias-preserving':
- noncss_comparison.md line 15 and lit_hardware.md line 114 (0.75/0.83/0.86%);
- lit_surface_family.md line 163 (as ranges).

The device form of bias (Z-dephasing memory with depolarizing gates) was simulated, and where the dephasing sits decides the outcome:
- per gate layer (ion models, hw_t): XYZ^2 loses;
- only during readout and reset, with perfect syndromes, at eta = 20 (hw_tr): XYZ^2 is nominally lower in BM pilots, and one point is established (p_T 4%, r = 6d; C33, C35);
- with f = 0.02 of p_T on every gate-layer idle site (m1 mixture, eta = 20), no edge is seen: R 1.02(7), Poisson errors, stat only (C37). 0 < f < 0.02 is untested.

Untested: bias at every location with a CX compiled through H (2505.17718). XYZ^2's values lie between XZZX's two non-bias-preserving compilations (CZ-only 0.73-0.80%, HBD 0.85-0.93%), so that model could produce anything from a loss to a tie (cross-model).

Gate set: CY = (I x S) CX (I x S^dag) is bias-preserving in the cat-qubit set. Treating CY and CZ as native favours both codes alike, apart from the omitted Z(pi/2) noise.

Untested; the expected directions are inference.

hw_tr's bulk is equivalent to repeated code capacity at a per-round p of about p_T: every syndrome is perfect, and noise acts in two half-layers per round. C35 is therefore measured code capacity with MDR time boundaries.

Noise:
- strong Z bias (biased100, purez) at p < 1e-3. The equal-qubit verdict is measured at p = 1e-3 to 3e-3 only. From p = 3e-3 to 2e-3, XYZ^2's Lambda grows faster as p falls: log-log slope 2.00(12) against 1.35(12) for XZZX under biased100, and 1.89(12) against 1.18(14) under purez (3.9 and 3.8 sigma, stat only; C6, C9). An exponent crossover below 1e-3 is not excluded (inference). At 99/97 qubits XYZ^2 is 10.4-10.7x worse under biased100 and 16.1-17.4x worse under purez (68% intervals about +-12%), with no trend in p, so a crossover in absolute error would need large d (inference);
- X- and Y-biased circuit noise;
- non-bias-preserving gates (C26);
- erasure and leakage;
- intra-link correlated ZZ dephasing;
- quasi-static dephasing, which could make a two-window layout up to 4x worse without an echo;
- amplitude damping with ZZ links (C28);
- hybrid hardware with unequal rates.

hw_tr-type models (C35, C37, C38):
- gate-layer dephasing fractions 0 < f < 0.02;
- each code at its own QEC-optimal readout time (Hetenyi-Wootton Eq. 14 with this repository's corner thresholds: about 7.3 us for XYZ^2, 7.9 us for XZZX);
- the exact maximum-fidelity point (p_T 3.3%, p_T2 3.2%, p_RR 1.6%);
- reset, single-qubit gate and readout-pair two-qubit errors;
- p_T < 3% without gate and readout error;
- eta between 10 and 20 with more shots;
- r = 1 MDR preparation at campaign standard, at p_T other than 4%, at other eta, at d = 3 and in the device-like mixtures. The one measured pair (p_T 4%, eta 20, 99/97 qubits) is an 18.5(7)x loss for XYZ^2 (stat only; C9, C35);
- d = 3 (35/31 qubits);
- Hetenyi-Wootton's 4d^2:3d^2 qubit pairing;
- a true two-window cycle;
- ZZ links;
- just-in-time or Z-basis link ancillas;
- XYZ^2 bp_corr and CFE;
- matching-class decoding at other p_T and sizes (one point measured, C23).

Hardware:
- neutral atoms;
- routing on degree-4 and degree-3 chips;
- the heavy-hex compilation of 2109.13308;
- Hetenyi-Wootton's square-lattice layout with SWAPs for both codes;
- shuttling spin architectures;
- helios_cb for the competitors;
- a per-gate timing model for ions.

Competitors:
- CSS (Z-basis preparation), honeycomb and XY at equal qubits in hw_tr;
- rectangular, co-prime and d^2-frame XZZX/XY with ML or tensor-network decoding in hw_tr (2112.06036 Fig. 7c shows near parity, 0-14%, against the d^2 frame at code capacity);
- a 5x10 rectangular patch (99 qubits) for r = 1 preparation (35-qubit and 95-qubit rectangles were run, C11);
- rectangular or tailored CSS under gate-level bias;
- Clifford-deformed and colour codes; repetition cat codes;
- 3aux under em3;
- competitor BP-OSD and Tesseract at fss;
- a built pipelined XZZX;
- the competitors' third hyb option;
- competitor runs at r = 4, 6-9 and 11-21.

Equal-qubit runs missing under hyb, the ions, em3, SI1000 memory, and hw_tr at campaign standard. Also missing: logical operations (lattice surgery, injection), which all touch the weak time boundary.

open_questions:
Does the hw_tr memory edge (C35) survive device realism at campaign standard? Needed:
- gate-layer dephasing fractions 0 < f < 0.02, and the gate-layer duration and echo scheme of a real spin device. The device's f lies anywhere from below 1e-3 to above 0.02 depending on the coherence model, and reaches 0.02 at layers of about 130 ns (maximum-fidelity readout) or 90 ns (QEC-optimal readout) if gate-layer dephasing adds coherently to the readout window (C37, C38);
- each code at its own QEC-optimal readout time (Hetenyi-Wootton Eq. 14: about 7.3 us for XYZ^2 and 7.9 us for XZZX with this repository's corners, 5.7 us and 7.5 us with their Table I), and the exact maximum-fidelity point (p_T2 3.2%, p_T 3.3%, p_RR 1.6%) (C30, C37, C38);
- reset, single-qubit gate and readout-pair two-qubit errors, and quasi-static dephasing (C30, C37, C38);
- eta = 10 at p_T 4-6%, eta between 10 and 20 with more shots, eta for realistic data T1 (T1 = 10 ms gives eta of about 70-100, where eta = 100 at p_T 3% shows no edge), and p_T below 3% (C30, C34, C35, C37);
- larger d to settle the size trend (Lambda 6.5(13) against a break-even of 5.5-5.8), and more r = 6d points with recorded seeds, to test whether the two XYZ^2 runs per basis share an offset (delta* 0.30-0.32 if they do) (C33, C35);
- Hetenyi-Wootton's 4d^2:3d^2 layout with a true two-window cycle and ZZ links. The crude bare-hw_tr estimate gives 0.96(7)x XZZX d = 5 at 100/75 qubits (C30, C37);
- just-in-time or Z-basis link ancillas (100 of XYZ^2's 254 gate-layer idle sites are waiting link ancillas) (C25, C30);
- a harsher preparation boundary at eta = 20. At eta = 10, phen with q = 0 gives a BM L_Y ratio of 1.44(9), a loss for delta up to 0.21, and a corr_gauge L_Y ratio of 1.91(11), a loss that holds (C9, C35);
- d = 3 (35/31 qubits) (C30);
- CSS-Z, honeycomb and XY at equal qubits, plus rectangular, co-prime and ML- or tensor-network-decoded d^2-frame surface patches (C30, C35);
- XYZ^2 with the eleven decoders not yet run under hw_tr (corr_links, seq_soft, seq_erasure, seq_match, bp_full, bp_corr, tesseract, cfe, cfe0, tnml, cfe_tn), and matching-class decoders at more points (C30, C35);
- r = 1 MDR preparation at campaign standard (the archived pilot pair gives an 18.5(7)x loss; C9, C30, C35).

What is the run-level offset delta of the BM pilots (C33)?
- Four of about 17 replicate sets exceed Poisson scatter at p < 0.05. In three of them one run or one pair of runs carries the excess.
- The estimates span about 0.065 (all 42 sets of statrev/homog.py, a lower estimate; 0.074 over its 35 XYZ^2 BM sets) to 0.37 (one flagged set with one degree of freedom).
- Seeds are time-based and were not recorded. Rerun with recorded seeds, and check whether runs of one batch share an offset.
- One competitor set also exceeds Poisson scatter: the phen_b10 r = 1, p = 3% XZZX corr X runs give delta 0.044, which changes no label (C33).
- delta decides whether the delta-dependent points count. Among them:
  - hw_tr p_T 6% at r = 3d (delta* 0.148), p_T 3% L_Y (0.075), 195 qubits (0.125), r = 6d better basis against better basis (0.227), and the r = 6d lead itself if its two XYZ^2 runs share one offset (0.30-0.32);
  - phen_b10 50/49 at 2% L_Y (0.13), 2.5% L_X (0.069) and 3% L_X (0.078), 18/16 against XZZX d = 4 (0.097), q = 0 18/16 (0.144) and q = 0 L_Y (0.213);
  - m1 better basis (0.100) and m1 without its outlying run (0.096);
  - run E (0.217);
  - hw_rr with BM (0.225);
  - phen 50/49 (0.189) and 18/16 (0.085);
  - the SI1000 BM comparisons against square patches at 35 qubits (0.12) and at 99/97 (0.19-0.22).

Strong Z bias at p below 1e-3 (C6, C9, C33). The equal-qubit loss to XZZX is measured only at p = 1e-3 to 3e-3. From 3e-3 to 2e-3 XYZ^2's Lambda grows faster than XZZX's (log-log slopes 2.00(12) against 1.35(12) for biased100 and 1.89(12) against 1.18(14) for purez, 3.9 and 3.85 sigma stat only), but the margin over the sqrt2 needed for exponent parity is 0.4 and 0.95 sigma. Run:
- XZZX at d = 13-15 and XYZ^2 at d >= 9 at p = 1e-3 and below;
- XYZ^2 BM at d >= 7 in both bases (Y-basis BM at d = 9 and p = 3e-3 was not run);
- CFE or Tesseract at r = 3d (one same-shot point suggests 2.0-2.5x decoder headroom for XYZ^2).
The d = 7 to 9 steps reach exponent parity at about 3e-4 to 5e-4 at their 68% upper ends, and the dof-0 lambda.csv fits put it far lower. These runs would also resolve the 1e-3 pairs at 323/337 and 483/449, which docs/data/campaign/eq/pairs.csv gives only as bounds.

Fixed-footprint r = 1 preparation (C10, C11, C33, C36):
- XYZ^2 d >= 9 (323 qubits and more) under SI1000, and XYZ^2 BM at d = 7;
- the other campaign noise models at 35-48 qubits (biased, ions away from the device points, em3, hyb, phen, phen_b10, the lr variants);
- SD6 at 35 qubits for a layout that serves both bases (an XYZ^2 d = 3 L_Y run and a 6x3 Z run);
- a 10x5 patch on 99 qubits;
- the routing cost of XYZ^2's degree-6 couplings on degree-4 or heavy-hex chips;
- a rectangular layout option in the repository's competitor_circuit, which takes a single distance (the pilots use usefulness_pilots/r6rev4/rect.py and c11r7/rect_c.py).

Comparison rules to fix before any headline table:
- one rule for isomorphic competitors, either pooling or the best single fit (it decides the SI1000 r = 1 MDR label and sd6_il r = 5; C3, C33);
- the matched-decoder (symmetric) table as the headline (usefulness_pilots/fairrev/sym.py; C24);
- a drift rule that also flags upward drift (C19, C24);
- competitor BP-OSD and Tesseract fits at fss (C2, C23, C24);
- one pilot significance rule with a fixed delta, and one (b) convention across claims (C33 applies f = 1.7 to single runs on either side; C11 and C28 apply it to XYZ^2 runs only) (C11, C28, C33);
- an fss systematic for extrapolation and drift (C33);
- the competitors' third hyb option, built, and a built pipelined XZZX in place of the sd6_noidle bound (C15, C24, C30);
- BM for CSS in the equal-qubit stage, and BM for XZZX at d >= 11 (C4, C8).

Coverage gaps (C30):
- equal-qubit runs under hyb, the ion models, em3 (against the honeycomb and 3aux), SI1000 memory, and hw_tr at campaign standard;
- competitor runs under helios_cb and at r = 4, 6-9 and 11-21;
- r = 1 thresholds for the spin corners hw_g, hw_g1q, hw_t and hw_rr (C10);
- a per-gate timing model for ions;
- neutral atoms with erasure and atom loss;
- routing on degree-4 and degree-3 chips, Hetenyi-Wootton's square-lattice layout with SWAPs for both codes, and a threshold study of the heavy-hex compilation in 2109.13308;
- non-bias-preserving gates (2505.17718), X- and Y-biased circuit noise, and amplitude damping with ZZ links (C26, C28);
- rectangular or tailored CSS under gate-level bias, and repetition cat codes (C7);
- logical operations (lattice surgery, injection), which all touch the weak time boundary.

Outputs not yet in docs/data/campaign/usefulness_pilots/:
- the hw_tr MWPM probes (XYZ^2 4.58e-3 to 4.61e-3, XZZX 3.29e-4) and the corr_gauge reruns up to 2.32(3)x; the corr_gauge probe itself is archived as cg_Y.txt and cg_X.txt (C23, C35);
- the repeat r = 1 runs in hw_tr and phen_b10 beyond r1_xyz2.out, r1_xzzx.out, r1_phen_xyz2.out and r1_phen_xzzx.out. They exist only as values recorded in the claim texts, and the phen_b10 L_X ratios quoted from them reach 10.7x (C10) or 11.2x (C31) depending on which recorded runs are paired (C9, C10, C31, C35);
- the rv/eqprobe.py MWPM outputs for hw_tr (C21);
- the XZZX d = 5 hw_tr runs at p = 3% behind the two-window estimate (C37);
- the hw_rr Stim boundary and layout checks (C20);
- sympair.py (C5) and the C6 parity-extrapolation script;
- the device-f arithmetic (C36, C37, C38).
The hw_tr XZZX-X crossing lies between p of about 0.19 and 0.31 and may be a saturation effect. Locating it needs more shots (C21).

material_omissions_last_round.text:
Scope of pilot error model (c) by decoder. (c) adds a run-level variance only to XYZ^2 BM runs. Every XYZ^2 pilot lead is BM, while the corr_gauge and MWPM losses are labelled on (a) and (b) alone. Applying (c) uniformly at delta 0.37 to every XYZ^2 run makes these 'losses that hold' delta-dependent:
- hw_rr corr_gauge and MWPM (delta* about 0.25);
- the SI1000 35-qubit both-bases L_Y loss (delta* about 0.14);
- SI1000 99/97 corr_gauge vs square CSS d = 7 (0.30-0.33);
- Helios vs CSS-X (0.34);
- phen_b10 q = 0 corr_gauge L_Y (0.21).
The SI1000 square-patch corr_gauge lead 0.664 also becomes delta-dependent (0.13). These survive: H2 (0.51), SD6 99/97 (0.70), SI1000 8x6, the 35-qubit rectangles, hw_tr r = 1, biased10 (1.3) and phen 98/100 (0.39). The exemption has measured support: hw_rr corr_gauge vs MWPM differ by 0.002 in ln, SI1000 MWPM si.log vs ovc differ by 0.94 sigma, and the biased10 corr_gauge sources agree within 0.4-1.2 sigma. C33 should state this as the basis of the rule, not 'untested', and name the affected labels. No verdict changes.

material_omissions_last_round.reason:
Recomputed from docs/data/campaign/eq/points_eps.csv (sd6, r = 3d, corr/corr_gauge), taking the weaker basis per code at each point:
- 1e-3: 15.2x (99/97), 470x (323/337: XYZ^2 X 2.487e-7 vs CSS X 5.294e-10);
- 2e-3: 9.5x, 118x, 283x;
- 3e-3: 7.53x (XYZ^2 Y 2.028e-3 vs CSS Z 2.694e-4), 76x, 129x.
That gives a range of 7.5-470x. eq.md fixes the basis per code, model and p, and in these cases that picks CSS's stronger basis or XYZ^2's stronger basis. The headline number changes, but the direction does not: every pair is a large loss. The upper end still rests on 2-3 CSS errors.

